---
import Layout from "../layouts/Layout.astro";
import MoreInCategory from "../components/MoreInCategory.astro";
import AuthorBio from "../components/AuthorBio.astro";
import { authorSchema } from "../data/authors";
import AuthorAvatar from "../components/AuthorAvatar.astro";
import TrustBadge from "../components/TrustBadge.astro";
import SocialProof from "../components/SocialProof.astro";
import { pageDates } from "../data/dates";

const moreInCategory = [
    { title: "Best Garage Floor Coating", url: "/best-garage-floor-coating/" },
    { title: "How to Prepare Concrete for Epoxy", url: "/how-to-prepare-concrete-for-epoxy/" },
    { title: "Epoxy vs Polyaspartic", url: "/epoxy-vs-polyaspartic-garage-floor/" },
];
const dates = pageDates(Astro.url.pathname, { published: "2026-09-06", modified: "2026-09-06" });
const title = "Epoxy Flooring: Types, Costs and Where It Works";
const description =
  "The three resin types, what each costs installed, and the rooms epoxy suits. Plus the moisture test that decides whether it bonds at all.";

const schema = {
  "@context": "https://schema.org",
  "@graph": [
    {
      "@type": "BreadcrumbList",
      itemListElement: [
        {
          "@type": "ListItem",
          position: 1,
          name: "Home",
          item: "https://www.thehonestreviewers.com/",
        },
        {
          "@type": "ListItem",
          position: 2,
          name: "Guides",
          item: "https://www.thehonestreviewers.com/best-of/",
        },
        {
          "@type": "ListItem",
          position: 3,
          name: "Epoxy Flooring",
          item: "https://www.thehonestreviewers.com/epoxy-flooring/",
        },
      ],
    },
    {
      "@type": "FAQPage",
      mainEntity: [
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "What is epoxy flooring?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "A two-part system: a resin and a hardener that react to form a rigid plastic layer bonded to concrete. It is not a paint that dries, it is a chemical reaction that cures, which is why mixing ratio and working time matter so much more than they do with paint.",
          },
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "How long does epoxy flooring last?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "A properly prepared 100% solids epoxy floor in a residential garage lasts ten to twenty years. Solvent-based products give three to five, and water-based ones one to three. The difference is mostly solids content and preparation rather than brand.",
          },
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "What is the difference between water-based and 100% solids epoxy?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "Solids content is how much of what you roll on stays on the floor. A 45 percent solids product loses over half its volume to evaporation and cures to a very thin film. 100% solids leaves everything behind, giving a far thicker and longer-lasting floor, at the cost of a pot life often as short as thirty minutes.",
          },
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "Can you put epoxy on a basement floor?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "Often, but test for moisture first. Basement slabs frequently have vapour driving up through them, and an impermeable coating traps it until the film lifts. Tape plastic sheeting to the bare slab for 24 to 48 hours and check for condensation before committing.",
          },
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "Can epoxy flooring go outside?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "Standard epoxy should not. Ultraviolet light ambers and chalks it within a season or two, and exterior slabs add freeze-thaw cycling that attacks the bond. Use a penetrating concrete sealer outdoors, which soaks into the slab rather than forming a film that can lift.",
          },
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "Do I need to grind the concrete before epoxy?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "It is the reliable option and what professionals use. Acid etching works only on bare, porous, untreated concrete, and fails invisibly on hard-troweled, sealed or oily slabs. Since epoxy bonds mechanically into surface texture, anything that leaves the slab smooth leaves it nothing to grip.",
          },
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "Why is my epoxy floor peeling?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "Almost always moisture from beneath or inadequate preparation. Moisture failure lifts the coating in sheets with clean concrete underneath. Prep failure usually starts at an edge or a wheel track. Either way the product is rarely at fault, and recoating without fixing the cause repeats the failure.",
          },
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "Is epoxy flooring worth it?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "On a sound, dry, properly prepared slab, yes, because a decade or two of service makes the cost per year low. On a slab that fails the moisture test or is cracked and unrepaired, tiles or roll-out matting are the better answer, since nothing bonds and so nothing can delaminate.",
          },
        },
      ],
    },
    {
      "@type": "Article",
      headline: title,
      description: description,
      author: authorSchema(),
      datePublished: dates.published,
      dateModified: dates.modified,
      publisher: { "@type": "Organization", name: "The Honest Reviewers" },
    },
  ],
};
---

<Layout
  title={title}
  description={description}
  ogType="article"
  article={{
    publishedTime: dates.published,
    modifiedTime: dates.modified,
    author: "Alex Rivers",
    section: "Home Improvement",
    tags: [
      "epoxy flooring",
      "epoxy floor coating",
      "concrete epoxy",
      "home improvement",
    ],
  }}
  jsonLd={schema}
  stickyCta={{
    text: "See Our Top Garage Floor Epoxy Picks",
    link: "/best-garage-floor-epoxy/",
  }}
>
  <article class="relative overflow-hidden">
    <!-- Hero -->
    <div class="bg-gray-900 py-16 lg:py-24 relative overflow-hidden">
      <div class="max-w-4xl mx-auto px-4 relative z-10 text-center">
        <div class="flex justify-center mb-6">
          <TrustBadge />
        </div>
        <div class="flex justify-center gap-3 mb-6">
          <span class="inline-flex items-center gap-1.5 px-3 py-1.5 rounded-full bg-[#FF4500]/20 text-[#FF4500] text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest border border-[#FF4500]/30">
            Complete Guide
          </span>
          <span class="inline-flex items-center gap-1.5 px-3 py-1.5 rounded-full bg-green-500/10 text-green-400 text-xs font-bold uppercase tracking-widest border border-green-500/20">
            Field Tested
          </span>
        </div>
        <h1 class="text-4xl lg:text-6xl font-black text-white leading-tight mb-8">
          Epoxy Flooring: <span class="text-[#38BDF8]">The Complete Guide</span>
        </h1>
        <p class="text-xl text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-8 max-w-3xl mx-auto">
          Most epoxy floors that fail were doomed before the first coat went down, by a slab that was never tested for moisture or never ground. The product is rarely the problem.
        </p>
        <div class="flex flex-col sm:flex-row gap-4 justify-center">
          <a
            href="#resin-types"
            class="px-8 py-4 bg-[#FF4500] hover:bg-[#E63E00] text-white font-black rounded-xl shadow-lg transition-all no-underline"
          >
            See the Resin Types
          </a>
          <a
            href="#faq"
            class="px-8 py-4 bg-white/10 hover:bg-white/20 text-white font-bold rounded-xl border border-white/10 no-underline"
          >
            Jump to the FAQ
          </a>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- Author & Meta -->
    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 pt-12">
      <div class="flex items-center gap-6 border-b border-gray-100 pb-8">
        <div class="flex items-center gap-3">
          <AuthorAvatar size={48} />
          <div>
            <p class="text-sm font-bold text-gray-900">Alex Rivers</p>
            <p class="text-xs text-gray-500 uppercase tracking-widest">Home Improvement Editor</p>
          </div>
        </div>
        <div class="h-10 w-px bg-gray-200 hidden sm:block"></div>
        <div class="hidden sm:block">
          <p class="text-sm font-bold text-gray-900">Last Updated</p>
          <p class="text-xs text-gray-500">{dates.display}</p>
        </div>
        <div class="hidden sm:block ml-auto">
          <SocialProof users="24.6k" />
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- Table of Contents -->
    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 py-10">
      <div class="bg-gray-50 border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8">
        <h2 class="text-lg font-black text-gray-900 mb-4 flex items-center gap-2">
          In This Guide
        </h2>
        <nav class="grid grid-cols-1 sm:grid-cols-2 gap-2">
          <a href="#resin-types" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">1. The Three Types</a>
          <a href="#where-it-works" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">2. Where It Works</a>
          <a href="#moisture-test" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">3. The Moisture Test</a>
          <a href="#prep" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">4. Preparation</a>
          <a href="#costs" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">5. Costs</a>
          <a href="#alternatives" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">6. Alternatives</a>
          <a href="#lifespan" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">7. Lifespan</a>
          <a href="#common-mistakes" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">8. Common Mistakes to Avoid</a>
          <a href="#faq" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">9. Frequently Asked Questions</a>
        </nav>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- Body -->
    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 pb-16 lg:pb-24">
      <div class="prose prose-lg prose-blue max-w-none text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
        <p class="text-2xl font-medium text-gray-900 mb-8 italic">
          Epoxy is two liquids that become a plastic. Mix a resin with a hardener and a chemical reaction produces a rigid layer bonded to concrete. Everything that separates a ten-year floor from a two-year one follows from that sentence.
        </p>

        <div id="resin-types" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            1. The Three Resin Types
          </h2>

          <p>
            Almost every disappointment in this category traces back to buying one type while expecting another. The word epoxy covers products that differ by an order of magnitude in thickness and life.
          </p>

          <div class="overflow-hidden border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl my-8">
            <div class="overflow-x-auto">
              <table class="w-full text-left">
                <thead class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-200">
                  <tr>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Type</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Solids</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Coat Thickness</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Working Time</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Realistic Life</th>
                  </tr>
                </thead>
                <tbody>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Water-based</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">~40-50%</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Very thin</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Longest</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">1-3 years</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Solvent-based</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">~50-70%</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Thin</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Moderate</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">3-5 years</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">100% solids</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">100%</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Thickest</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Shortest, often 30-40 min</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">10-20 years</td>
                  </tr>
                </tbody>
              </table>
            </div>
          </div>

          <p>
            Solids content is the number that matters, because it tells you how much of what you roll on stays on the floor. A water-based product at 45 percent solids loses more than half its volume to evaporation, so a coat that looks generous while wet cures to a film you can barely feel.
          </p>

          <p>
            That is not a scam, it is a different product for a different job. Water-based epoxy is easier to apply, more forgiving of a damp slab, cheap, and genuinely fine for a basement or a low-traffic utility room. It is not what people picture when they imagine an epoxy garage floor.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-3">The Trade Nobody Warns You About</p>
            <p class="text-xl font-bold text-white mb-3">100% solids gives you the floor and takes away the time.</p>
            <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed m-0">The thickest, longest-lasting epoxy is also the least forgiving to apply, because there is no solvent to slow the reaction. Pot life is often thirty to forty minutes from the moment you mix, and when it starts to gel it does so in the bucket regardless of how much floor is left. That single fact is why plenty of DIY 100% solids jobs go wrong, and why professionals mix in small batches.</p>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div id="where-it-works" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            2. Where Epoxy Works, and Where It Does Not
          </h2>

          <p>
            Epoxy is exceptional on interior concrete that stays dry and out of direct sun. Its two genuine weaknesses, moisture from beneath and ultraviolet light, decide most of this table.
          </p>

          <div class="overflow-hidden border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl my-8">
            <div class="overflow-x-auto">
              <table class="w-full text-left">
                <thead class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-200">
                  <tr>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Location</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Suitable?</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">The Deciding Factor</th>
                  </tr>
                </thead>
                <tbody>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Attached garage</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Yes, the classic use</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Test for moisture first</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Basement</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Usually</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Vapour drive is common; test before committing</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Workshop and utility room</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Yes</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Chemical and abrasion resistance suit it well</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Commercial kitchen and retail</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Yes</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Usually a professional specification</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Detached garage on a slab</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Often</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Slabs without a vapour barrier are the risk</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Outdoor patio or driveway</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">No</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">UV ambers it and freeze-thaw lifts it</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Anywhere with standing water beneath</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">No</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">It will delaminate whatever you do</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Over failing or spalling concrete</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">No</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">The coating is only as sound as the slab</td>
                  </tr>
                </tbody>
              </table>
            </div>
          </div>

          <p>
            The outdoor row is worth stating plainly because it is the most common misapplication. Standard epoxy ambers and chalks under ultraviolet light, going yellow within a season or two of direct sun, and on an exterior slab it also has to survive freeze-thaw cycling that will find any weakness in the bond. Driveways and patios want a penetrating sealer instead, which sits in the concrete rather than on it.
          </p>

          <p>
            Indoors, the same UV weakness shows up in a narrower way: a garage with a large south-facing window or a door left open all summer can amber in the sunlit strip alone. A polyaspartic or polyurethane topcoat solves it, and it is one of the main reasons those coats exist.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="moisture-test" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            3. The Test That Decides Everything
          </h2>

          <p>
            If you take one thing from this guide, take this. Concrete on grade wicks moisture from the ground continuously, and that vapour has to leave somewhere. Coat the slab with an impermeable film and the vapour collects underneath until it pushes the coating off.
          </p>

          <p>
            The failure looks like bubbling, then patches that lift cleanly with no epoxy stuck to the concrete underneath. People blame the product. The product was fine; it was applied to a slab that was pushing water at it.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">The Plastic Sheet Test</h3>

          <ul class="space-y-2 my-6">
            <li class="flex items-start gap-2 text-gray-700">
              <svg class="w-5 h-5 text-[#1E90FF] mt-0.5 flex-shrink-0" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" viewBox="0 0 24 24"><path stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" stroke-width="2" d="M9 12l2 2 4-4"></path></svg>
              <span>Tape a square of plastic sheeting, roughly 18 by 18 inches, tightly to the bare slab on all four edges.</span>
            </li>
            <li class="flex items-start gap-2 text-gray-700">
              <svg class="w-5 h-5 text-[#1E90FF] mt-0.5 flex-shrink-0" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" viewBox="0 0 24 24"><path stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" stroke-width="2" d="M9 12l2 2 4-4"></path></svg>
              <span>Leave it for 24 to 48 hours, longer in cool weather.</span>
            </li>
            <li class="flex items-start gap-2 text-gray-700">
              <svg class="w-5 h-5 text-[#1E90FF] mt-0.5 flex-shrink-0" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" viewBox="0 0 24 24"><path stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" stroke-width="2" d="M9 12l2 2 4-4"></path></svg>
              <span>Lift it and look. Condensation on the underside, or a dark damp patch on the concrete, means vapour is coming through.</span>
            </li>
            <li class="flex items-start gap-2 text-gray-700">
              <svg class="w-5 h-5 text-[#1E90FF] mt-0.5 flex-shrink-0" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" viewBox="0 0 24 24"><path stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" stroke-width="2" d="M9 12l2 2 4-4"></path></svg>
              <span>Test several spots, especially near walls, in corners, and anywhere the slab meets earth.</span>
            </li>
            <li class="flex items-start gap-2 text-gray-700">
              <svg class="w-5 h-5 text-[#1E90FF] mt-0.5 flex-shrink-0" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" viewBox="0 0 24 24"><path stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" stroke-width="2" d="M9 12l2 2 4-4"></path></svg>
              <span>A dry sheet everywhere is a good sign but not a guarantee, since conditions change seasonally.</span>
            </li>
            <li class="flex items-start gap-2 text-gray-700">
              <svg class="w-5 h-5 text-[#1E90FF] mt-0.5 flex-shrink-0" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" viewBox="0 0 24 24"><path stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" stroke-width="2" d="M9 12l2 2 4-4"></path></svg>
              <span>For anything expensive, a calcium chloride or relative humidity test gives a number instead of an impression.</span>
            </li>
          </ul>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-3">If the Test Comes Back Wet</p>
            <p class="text-xl font-bold text-white mb-3">You have three options, and one of them is not "coat it anyway".</p>
            <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed m-0">Fix the source where you can: grading, gutters and downpipes moving water away from the slab. Use a moisture-tolerant system, meaning a purpose-made vapour barrier primer or a breathable coating rather than standard epoxy. Or choose a covering that does not need to bond at all, such as interlocking tiles or roll-out matting, which let the slab breathe underneath.</p>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div id="prep" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            4. Preparation Is the Whole Job
          </h2>

          <p>
            Epoxy bonds mechanically, gripping into the texture of the concrete. A smooth, sealed or contaminated slab gives it nothing to grip, which is why preparation accounts for most of the labour on any professional job and most of the failures on amateur ones.
          </p>

          <div class="overflow-hidden border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl my-8">
            <div class="overflow-x-auto">
              <table class="w-full text-left">
                <thead class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-200">
                  <tr>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Method</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">What It Does</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Verdict</th>
                  </tr>
                </thead>
                <tbody>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Diamond grinding</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Opens the surface mechanically</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">The reliable choice, and what pros use</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Shot blasting</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Abrades with steel media</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Excellent on large commercial areas</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Acid etching</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Opens the surface chemically</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Works on some slabs, inconsistent on others</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Pressure washing alone</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Cleans only</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Not preparation; it changes no profile</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Degreasing alone</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Removes contamination</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Necessary, but not sufficient</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Nothing</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Nothing</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">The most common reason a floor peels</td>
                  </tr>
                </tbody>
              </table>
            </div>
          </div>

          <p>
            Acid etching is the method most kits include and the one that most often disappoints. It works on a bare, porous, untreated slab. It does very little on a hard-troweled floor, a previously sealed one, or one with oil in it, and there is no obvious way to tell you have failed until the coating lifts months later. Grinding removes that uncertainty, and a rented grinder for a day costs far less than doing the floor twice.
          </p>

          <p>
            Oil is the other prep problem, because it soaks into porous concrete and no amount of scrubbing lifts what has penetrated. It needs a degreaser or an absorbent poultice that draws it back out, and any oil left in the slab is a spot where the epoxy simply will not bond. Our guides to <a href="/how-to-prepare-concrete-for-epoxy/">preparing concrete for epoxy</a> and <a href="/how-to-get-oil-out-of-concrete/">getting oil out of concrete</a> cover both in detail.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="costs" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            5. What It Actually Costs
          </h2>

          <p>
            Cost ranges in this category are wide because they cover genuinely different products and levels of preparation. These are per square foot, which is how the trade quotes.
          </p>

          <div class="overflow-hidden border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl my-8">
            <div class="overflow-x-auto">
              <table class="w-full text-left">
                <thead class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-200">
                  <tr>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Approach</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Typical Per Sq Ft</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">What You Get</th>
                  </tr>
                </thead>
                <tbody>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">DIY water-based kit</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Lowest</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">A thin film, a season or two of looks</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">DIY 100% solids kit</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Low to moderate</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">A real floor, if prep and timing go right</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Professional epoxy</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Moderate</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Ground slab, correct thickness, warranty</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Professional epoxy with topcoat</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Moderate to high</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">UV and abrasion protection over the epoxy</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Professional polyaspartic</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Highest</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">One-day install, best UV stability</td>
                  </tr>
                </tbody>
              </table>
            </div>
          </div>

          <p>
            The number that actually matters is cost per year of service rather than cost today. A cheap kit that needs redoing every second year is not cheaper than a system that lasts a decade; it is more expensive and it costs you the weekend each time. Our <a href="/garage-floor-coating-cost/">garage floor coating cost guide</a> works through the per-square-foot pricing and the DIY versus professional gap with real figures.
          </p>

          <p>
            Where DIY genuinely wins is a sound, dry, well-prepared slab in a modest garage where you own or can rent a grinder and you are comfortable working to a thirty-minute pot life. Where it genuinely loses is a large area, a slab that needs real repair, or any job where you would be learning on the only floor you have.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="alternatives" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            6. The Alternatives Worth Knowing
          </h2>

          <p>
            Epoxy is not automatically the answer, and three alternatives beat it in specific circumstances.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Polyaspartic and Polyurea</h3>

          <p>
            Both cure far faster than epoxy, tolerate a wider temperature range, and are genuinely UV stable rather than merely protected. That combination is why professional one-day garage floor installations use them. The trade is cost and an even shorter working time, which makes them difficult to apply well as a first attempt. Our comparisons of <a href="/epoxy-vs-polyaspartic-garage-floor/">epoxy versus polyaspartic</a> and <a href="/epoxy-vs-polyurea-garage-floor-coating/">epoxy versus polyurea</a> cover where each wins.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Interlocking Tiles and Roll-Out Matting</h3>

          <p>
            The honest answer for a slab that fails the moisture test, or one that is cracked and unattractive but structurally fine. Nothing bonds, so nothing delaminates, and the slab breathes underneath. They go down in an afternoon, they can be lifted and replaced individually, and they take a cracked floor out of view without repairing it. See our guide to <a href="/best-garage-floor-tiles/">garage floor tiles</a>.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Concrete Sealer</h3>

          <p>
            A penetrating sealer soaks into the slab rather than forming a film on top. It cannot delaminate because there is nothing to peel, it does not trap vapour, and it works outdoors where epoxy does not. It also will not give you a glossy showroom floor. For driveways, patios and any exterior concrete, this is the correct product rather than a compromise. See our <a href="/best-concrete-sealer/">concrete sealer guide</a>.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="lifespan" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            7. How Long It Lasts, and What Ends It
          </h2>

          <p>
            A properly specified and properly installed 100% solids epoxy floor in a residential garage is a ten to twenty year proposition. Almost nothing ends one early except the failures already described.
          </p>

          <ul class="space-y-2 my-6">
            <li class="flex items-start gap-2 text-gray-700">
              <svg class="w-5 h-5 text-[#1E90FF] mt-0.5 flex-shrink-0" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" viewBox="0 0 24 24"><path stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" stroke-width="2" d="M9 12l2 2 4-4"></path></svg>
              <span>Moisture from beneath, which lifts the coating in sheets with clean concrete underneath.</span>
            </li>
            <li class="flex items-start gap-2 text-gray-700">
              <svg class="w-5 h-5 text-[#1E90FF] mt-0.5 flex-shrink-0" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" viewBox="0 0 24 24"><path stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" stroke-width="2" d="M9 12l2 2 4-4"></path></svg>
              <span>Inadequate preparation, which shows up as peeling that starts at an edge or a wheel track.</span>
            </li>
            <li class="flex items-start gap-2 text-gray-700">
              <svg class="w-5 h-5 text-[#1E90FF] mt-0.5 flex-shrink-0" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" viewBox="0 0 24 24"><path stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" stroke-width="2" d="M9 12l2 2 4-4"></path></svg>
              <span>Hot tyre pickup, where warm tyres lift a thin or poorly cured coating; a hazard of thin films, not thick ones.</span>
            </li>
            <li class="flex items-start gap-2 text-gray-700">
              <svg class="w-5 h-5 text-[#1E90FF] mt-0.5 flex-shrink-0" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" viewBox="0 0 24 24"><path stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" stroke-width="2" d="M9 12l2 2 4-4"></path></svg>
              <span>UV ambering in sunlit strips, which is cosmetic rather than structural and preventable with a topcoat.</span>
            </li>
            <li class="flex items-start gap-2 text-gray-700">
              <svg class="w-5 h-5 text-[#1E90FF] mt-0.5 flex-shrink-0" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" viewBox="0 0 24 24"><path stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" stroke-width="2" d="M9 12l2 2 4-4"></path></svg>
              <span>Impact damage from dropped tools, which chips it and is a fair trade for a rigid surface.</span>
            </li>
            <li class="flex items-start gap-2 text-gray-700">
              <svg class="w-5 h-5 text-[#1E90FF] mt-0.5 flex-shrink-0" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" viewBox="0 0 24 24"><path stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" stroke-width="2" d="M9 12l2 2 4-4"></path></svg>
              <span>Harsh chemical spills left to sit, including some ice melts tracked in over a winter.</span>
            </li>
          </ul>

          <p>
            Note how many of those are decided before installation rather than during service. That is the shape of this category: the floor's life is mostly determined in the day of preparation, and very little of it is determined by how you treat it afterwards. Our guide to <a href="/how-long-does-a-garage-epoxy-floor-last/">how long an epoxy floor lasts</a> puts real numbers to each system.
          </p>

          <p>
            Maintenance is genuinely light. Sweep, mop with a neutral cleaner, and avoid harsh solvents and abrasive pads that dull the finish. The one seasonal item worth knowing is ice melt, which some products dislike, so rinsing tracked-in slush rather than letting it dry on is worth the trouble. See <a href="/how-to-clean-epoxy-garage-floor/">cleaning an epoxy garage floor</a>.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- Common Mistakes -->
        <div id="common-mistakes" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            8. Common Mistakes to Avoid
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Skipping the Moisture Test</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Concrete on grade wicks vapour from the ground continuously, and an impermeable coating traps it until it pushes the film off. Tape plastic sheeting to the bare slab for 24 to 48 hours and look for condensation underneath. It costs nothing and it is the single test that decides whether any of the rest is worth doing.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Acid Etching a Slab That Needs Grinding</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Etching works on bare, porous, untreated concrete and does very little on a hard-troweled, previously sealed or oil-contaminated floor. There is no obvious sign you have failed until the coating lifts months later. Grinding removes that uncertainty, and a day's grinder rental costs less than doing the floor twice.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Buying Water-Based and Expecting a Showroom Floor</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                A product at 45 percent solids loses more than half its volume to evaporation, so it cures to a film you can barely feel. It is a reasonable low-traffic basement coating and it is not what people picture. If you want the thick glossy floor, you want 100% solids and the thirty-minute pot life that comes with it.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Mixing More 100% Solids Epoxy Than You Can Spread</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                There is no solvent to slow the reaction, so pot life is often thirty to forty minutes from the moment the two parts meet, and it gels in the bucket whether or not there is floor left. Mix in small batches and have everything staged before you start, which is exactly what professionals do.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Putting Standard Epoxy Outdoors</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Ultraviolet light ambers and chalks it within a season or two, and an exterior slab adds freeze-thaw cycling that finds any weakness in the bond. Driveways and patios want a penetrating sealer that sits in the concrete rather than a film on top of it.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- FAQ -->
        <div id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            9. Frequently Asked Questions
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6">
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What is epoxy flooring?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                A two-part system: a resin and a hardener that react to form a rigid plastic layer bonded to concrete. It is not a paint that dries, it is a chemical reaction that cures, which is why mixing ratio and working time matter so much more than they do with paint.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How long does epoxy flooring last?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                A properly prepared 100% solids epoxy floor in a residential garage lasts ten to twenty years. Solvent-based products give three to five, and water-based ones one to three. The difference is mostly solids content and preparation rather than brand.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What is the difference between water-based and 100% solids epoxy?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Solids content is how much of what you roll on stays on the floor. A 45 percent solids product loses over half its volume to evaporation and cures to a very thin film. 100% solids leaves everything behind, giving a far thicker and longer-lasting floor, at the cost of a pot life often as short as thirty minutes.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Can you put epoxy on a basement floor?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Often, but test for moisture first. Basement slabs frequently have vapour driving up through them, and an impermeable coating traps it until the film lifts. Tape plastic sheeting to the bare slab for 24 to 48 hours and check for condensation before committing.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Can epoxy flooring go outside?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Standard epoxy should not. Ultraviolet light ambers and chalks it within a season or two, and exterior slabs add freeze-thaw cycling that attacks the bond. Use a penetrating concrete sealer outdoors, which soaks into the slab rather than forming a film that can lift.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Do I need to grind the concrete before epoxy?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                It is the reliable option and what professionals use. Acid etching works only on bare, porous, untreated concrete, and fails invisibly on hard-troweled, sealed or oily slabs. Since epoxy bonds mechanically into surface texture, anything that leaves the slab smooth leaves it nothing to grip.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Why is my epoxy floor peeling?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Almost always moisture from beneath or inadequate preparation. Moisture failure lifts the coating in sheets with clean concrete underneath. Prep failure usually starts at an edge or a wheel track. Either way the product is rarely at fault, and recoating without fixing the cause repeats the failure.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Is epoxy flooring worth it?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                On a sound, dry, properly prepared slab, yes, because a decade or two of service makes the cost per year low. On a slab that fails the moisture test or is cracked and unrepaired, tiles or roll-out matting are the better answer, since nothing bonds and so nothing can delaminate.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- Related Guides -->
        <div class="mt-16">
