            </a>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- Closing -->
        <div class="mt-16 bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-8">
          <h2 class="text-2xl font-black text-white mb-4">The Bottom Line</h2>
          <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4">
            Epoxy is a two-part chemical reaction rather than a paint, and solids content is the specification that decides everything. 100% solids gives a ten to twenty year floor with a thirty-minute pot life. Water-based at 45 percent solids loses more than half its volume to evaporation and gives you one to three years.
          </p>
          <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4">
            Before any of that, test the slab. Tape plastic sheeting down for 24 to 48 hours and look for condensation underneath, because concrete on grade pushes vapour upward and an impermeable film traps it until it lifts. A wet result is not a reason to coat it anyway; it is a reason to fix the drainage, use a moisture-tolerant system, or lay tiles that never bond at all.
          </p>
          <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4">
            Then prepare properly. Epoxy grips into surface texture, so grinding is the reliable option and acid etching fails invisibly on hard-troweled, sealed or oily slabs. Almost every floor that peels was decided on the day of preparation rather than during its service life, which is why professionals spend most of the job on the concrete rather than the coating.
          </p>
        </div>


